% Chapter 71 of the tariff (Section XIV): natural or cultured pearls,
% precious or semiprecious stones, precious metals, metals clad with precious
% metal, and articles thereof; imitation jewelry; coin — all eighteen
% headings (7101 to 7118) and their 55 six-digit subheadings. A library: it
% includes chapter_39.le (and through it gri.le), because Chapter 39 note
% 2(r) and Chapter 71 note 1 decide between a jewel of plastics and an
% article of plastics; tariff.le includes it.
%
% Text: HTSUS Revision 23 (2026), Chapter 71 notes 1 to 11 and subheading
% notes 1 to 3, Additional U.S. note 1, Section XV notes 3 and 5 (base
% metals), and the terms of the headings and subheadings
% (https://hts.usitc.gov/); the subheadings are the six-digit HS ones. US
% Customs rulings where the notes leave a word open (cubic zirconia is a
% synthetic stone; an article worn as jewelry; titanium is a base metal).
%
% How the chapter is read. The facts of a good are what it is (its kind: a
% ring, a gemstone, an ingot, a coin...), what its body is made of ("the
% metal of G is sterling silver" — a named metal or alloy, never the
% plating, which note 6 does not count as precious metal), what covers it
% by mechanical means ("G is clad with gold", note 7),
% and the gems it carries ("the gem of G is cubic zirconia"). The notes then
% decide:
%   PEARLS AND STONES (7101-7105): a loose gem by its group — pearls,
%   diamonds, other natural stones, synthetic stones — and whether it is
%   worked; dust or powder of gems.
%   PRECIOUS METALS (7106-7112): silver, gold, platinum and the platinum
%   metals (note 4) and their alloys (note 5: 2 percent of a precious metal
%   is enough, platinum before gold before silver), unwrought, semi-
%   manufactured or in powder (Additional U.S. note 1); base metal clad with
%   precious metal (note 7); waste and scrap (note 8: heading 7112 only).
%   ARTICLES (7113-7118): an article of jewelry (note 9: small objects of
%   personal adornment) of precious metal or of metal clad with precious
%   metal is of 7113, unless the precious metal is a minor constituent only
%   (note 2(a)); an article that incorporates pearls or stones and no more
%   than minor precious metal is of 7116 (note 2(b)); an article of jewelry
%   that incorporates neither pearls, stones nor precious metal (plating
%   aside) is imitation jewelry, 7117 (note 11) — of base metal (7117.11,
%   7117.19) or of other materials (7117.90).
% Chapter 71 note 1 puts in this chapter every article consisting wholly or
% partly of pearls, stones or precious metal; Chapter 39 note 2(r) sends
% imitation jewelry of 7117 out of Chapter 39. Both are written as
% exclusions from the headings of Chapter 39 (below): a bracelet of plastics
% worn as jewelry is of 7117, not 3926; a band of plastics that is not
% principally used to adorn the body (CBP, HQ 967922) stays in 3926. Chapter
% 71 note 3(g) excludes goods of Section XI. The other exclusions of note 3
% name headings outside the model (watches, toys, works of art...): a claim
% that a good belongs there is answered by the office (gri.le).
%
% Not modelled: the value bands and the statistical lines below six digits;
% an article incorporating both pearls and stones (7116.10 or 7116.20 by its
% essential character, which no rule here decides).
%
% Provenance: every rule and table is labelled and cites the passage it
% encodes; the documents say where their text is (sources/), so the editor
% shows each passage and the verifier checks every quotation.
%
% DISCLAIMER. This program is an example of Logical English. It is provided
% "as is", without warranty of any kind, express or implied, including any
% warranty that it is accurate, complete, up to date or fit for a particular
% purpose. It is not an official reading of the tariff or of the rulings it
% cites, it is not a binding classification ruling, and it is not legal or
% customs advice. Its answers may be wrong: do not rely on them for a real
% import, export or declaration; consult the official texts, the customs
% authority and a qualified professional. Its authors accept no liability for
% any loss or damage arising from its use.

the target language is: prolog.

the templates are:
    *a family* is a family of chapter seventy-one goods.
    the metal of *a thing* is *a metal*; undefined.
    *a thing* is clad with *a metal*; undefined.
    *a thing* has precious metal only as minor constituents; undefined.
    the gem of *a thing* is *a gem*; undefined.
    *a good* is unworked; undefined.
    *a good* is unsorted; undefined.
    *a good* is of industrial quality; undefined.
    *a good* is monetary gold; undefined.
    *a good* is legal tender; undefined.
    *a good* is not principally used to adorn the body; undefined.
    *a metal* is a precious metal of the group *a group*.
    *a metal* is a base metal.
    *a gem* is a gem of the group *a group*.
    *a group* is a gem group of heading *a heading*.
    *a group* is a group of pearls.
    *a group* is a group of stones.
    *a kind* is a form of metal *a form*.
    *a thing* has precious metal of the group *a group*.
    the precious metal group of *a thing* is *a group*.
    *a thing* is of metal clad with precious metal.
    *a thing* contains precious metal beyond minor constituents.
    *a thing* is of base metal.
    *a thing* incorporates pearls.
    *a thing* incorporates stones.
    *a thing* incorporates pearls or stones.
    *a good* is an article of jewelry.
    *a good* is an article of jewelry or a part thereof.
    *a good* is an article of chapter seventy-one.
    *a good* is imitation jewelry.
    the gem class of *a good* is *a class*.
    the gem state of *a good* is *a state*.
    the metal class of *a good* is *a class*.
    the metal form of *a good* is *a form*.
    the article class of *a good* in heading *a heading* is *a class*.
    the subheading in heading *a heading* for *a class* in state *a state* is *a code* under table gems.
    the subheading in heading *a heading* for *a class* in form *a form* is *a code* under table metals.
    the subheading in heading *a heading* for *a class* is *a code* under table articles.

